@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — the protocol that takes a generalization off the untested queue
# and refutes it in the same run
#
# The claim examined is pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment, which had two witnesses,
# no protocol, and therefore stood on the queue SPEC §16 derives from the absence
# of a pm:Testing node. It named its own falsifying case and it named the class of
# corpus to look in. Both were met, from a corpus nobody had opened.
#
# The falsifier asked for a transmission whose practice produces such appearances,
# whose earliest witness treats them as reliable evidence of attainment, and which
# names no way of telling a true one from a counterfeit; and it said the candidates
# are the visionary literatures that grade experiences by degree of light. The
# Yogāvacara's Manual grades the five jhānas by degree of light — firefly, the
# moon's halo, a star, the sun's halo, white — and names no counterfeit. It is the
# falsifier's own candidate class, met exactly.
#
# The sharp part is where it comes from. One of the two witnesses of the claim is
# the Visuddhimagga's ten imperfections of insight, the Theravāda commentary's
# answer to how a practitioner mistakes a state for a result. The refutation comes
# from a Theravāda meditation manual of the same lineage, whose ordination line
# was fetched from Siam in the middle of the eighteenth century, and which grades
# by inner light in far more detail than the commentary and supplies no
# imperfection at all. The claim was not broken by a distant corpus. It was broken
# from inside one of its own witnesses' traditions.
#

pmp:TheSignAndTheCounterfeitExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a transmission measuring progress by inner appearances always names a counterfeit"@en, "Ob eine Überlieferung, die den Fortschritt an inneren Erscheinungen misst, immer eine Fälschung benennt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that transmissions measuring progress by inner appearances name those appearances as the chief obstacle to further progress, on the ground that the practitioner takes them for the attainment, and place the mark of the counterfeit in the circumstances of the experience rather than in its intensity. The protocol is a reading protocol: what is claimed is a regularity in what transmissions say, so it fails or holds against texts and not against practitioners."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission whose practice produces inner appearances of the kind described, whose earliest witness treats them as reliable evidence of attainment, and which names no way of telling a true appearance from a counterfeit. The condition is the one the claim named for itself. It has been met."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched on 2026-09-26 in the Yogāvacara's Manual, read complete at the wording in Woodward's translation of 1916, for any way of telling a true appearance from a counterfeit and for any statement that an appearance is an obstacle. The whole work was read and then searched case-insensitively for counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, imperfection, obstacle, hindrance, defilement, upakkilesa, danger, beware, must not, and for the negative forms of appearing. Counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, obstacle, hindrance and defilement do not occur in the manual at all. Imperfection occurs once as a translator's gloss on a technical term and not as a state of the practice. Danger occurs five times: once in the translator's theosophical footnote and four times inside the similes of the ten forms of knowledge, where the danger is the content of the insight and not a hazard of the practice. The search is recorded at pmp:BoranTerminusSearch and pmp:BoranPericulumSearch in examinations/searches-boran-kammatthana.ttl."@en ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "A second search, over the compiled holding rather than the work, asked whether the holding already carried a transmission of this kind with no counterfeit, so that the case would not be the first. Every node whose definition names a colour was read, thirty-six of them, and every node whose definition names a sign or an appearance in the traditions that grade by them. It did not: pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen announces inner colours in advance and states the authenticity test the claim predicts — that the sense distinguishing truth from deception must be continually cultivated and the pupil must examine whether he has fallen out of his equilibrium; pmc:NeidanZhengyan announces its sequence in advance and the same work carries the difficulties and the demons in the adjacent chapter; the desert discernment literature and the Visuddhimagga are the claim's own two witnesses. So the manual is the first case of its kind in this holding and the claim held against everything that had been entered before this run."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26; the reading protocol is the falsifier's own and the corpus that met it was chosen because the falsifier named its class" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-26"^^xsd:date ;
    skos:note "What the case is, in the transmission's own terms. The manual grades the five raptures by a triad of colours each, and the five jhānas by a single ascending scale of light: the ecstasy of the first jhāna is like a firefly, of the second like the halo of the moon, of the third like the colour of the medicine star, of the fourth like the halo of the sun, of the fifth like white flowers. That is the whole criterion by which the stages are distinguished. The appearances are announced before the practitioner sits — the formula of request names the state he is to attain and the rubric that follows states which element appears and in which colours — and they are stated in the indicative, as what appears to him, with no conditional and no alternative branch anywhere in the work. The nodes are pmc:BoranPancapitiVanna and pmc:BoranPancajhanaVanna in traditions/boran-kammatthana.ttl."@en ;
    skos:note "What the refutation costs the claim, stated precisely, because a refutation that is allowed to cost too much is as bad as one that is allowed to cost nothing. Both halves of the claim fail against this case and they fail differently. The first half says such transmissions name the appearances as the chief obstacle to further progress; this one names no obstacle of any kind, so the first half fails by absence. The second half says the mark of the counterfeit is placed in the circumstances rather than the intensity; this one has no counterfeit, so the second half is not contradicted but vacuous. The claim's own note said the second half is the part that could fail and is therefore the part worth having, and it identified the failure mode as a tradition that measured the counterfeit by intensity. That is not what happened. What happened is a tradition with no counterfeit at all, and it grades by intensity — of light — without ever using the grading to reject anything."@en ;
    skos:note "What survives, and it is a narrower claim that this protocol does not write into the graph, because a claim is corrected where it is examined and a tidied sentence would hide the case. The two original witnesses are both commentarial or directorial literature: a handbook of discernment and a commentary on the path, both written to instruct somebody about somebody else's experience. The refuting case is a liturgy, written to be recited by the practitioner himself while he does the thing. That is the distinction a successor statement should try: the counterfeit is named where a transmission addresses a supervisor and not where it addresses the operator. If that holds, the claim was about a genre and not about inner appearances, and the two witnesses were selected by the genre without anybody noticing."@en ;
    skos:note "The lineage makes the case stronger than a distant one would have been, and it is worth saying why rather than leaving it as a coincidence. The claim's second witness is the Visuddhimagga's ten imperfections of insight, entered in this holding from Theravāda XII. The refuting manual belongs to the transmission whose ordination line was fetched from Siam and returned to Ceylon in 1753, uses the Pali vocabulary of jhāna, kasiṇa, nimitta, appanā and upacāra throughout, and cites the same canonical scheme of ten insights. A holding that had only asked distant corpora would have concluded that the Theravāda names its counterfeits; what it names is one text's counterfeits, and another text of the same lineage names none."@en ;
    skos:note "The one thing the transmission did say about danger it did not say in writing, and the protocol records it because it bears on where such rules live rather than on whether they exist. A Sinhala informant in 1916 held that the practice must not be taught by a guru who is not fully versed in it and able to see clairvoyantly what effect each meditation is having on his pupil, and named a pupil who went mad and died. That is at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako with pm:fieldAttestation and its gathering named. So the last holders of the practice carried a grave caution and the book they held carried none, which suggests that the successor statement should ask not only which genre names the counterfeit but whether the rule was ever in a book at all."@en ;
    skos:note "Where the protocol should go next, and it is a named address rather than a class. The Siamese and Khmer branches of the same transmission are published in part and were not opened: the pīti section of the Kammatthan Matchima Baep Lamdap from Wat Ratchasittharam, the manual ascribed to King Taksin of 1775, and the Khmer material edited by Bizot, of which one text is reported to state outright that the embryonic objects must be red like a ball of blood and that if another colour is mixed with it the form cannot be born. If that is at the wording, the same transmission holds both arrangements — a colour condition that can fail in one branch and none in another — and the successor statement can be tested inside one lineage instead of across the holding."@en .
